---
// REVIEWS BY MARKET — three offices, three real review profiles, side by side.
//
// THE ANGLE IS THE THING WE CAN SUBSTANTIATE. An anonymous wall of quotes is what every contractor
// site has and it proves nothing; three named offices each with their own Google profile, their own
// star average and their own review count is a claim a visitor can check in one click. It is also
// simply true of this business in a way it is not true of a franchise with one national number.
//
// ── THE CEILING IS FIVE PER MARKET AND THE DESIGN ADMITS IT ───────────────────────────────────
//
// The Places API returns at most five reviews per place. No pagination, no sort parameter. Fifteen
// across three markets is the maximum this section can ever show, so it is built as three readable
// columns rather than a grid that looks broken at fifteen cards and empty at three. A market with
// two reviews still reads as finished.
//
// ── THE GATE. THIS IS THE PART THAT MATTERS MOST ──────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The claims gate exists because the prototype shipped three invented testimonials — "Sarah M.",
// "Dave R.", "Priya K." — under a heading claiming they were local homeowners, on two different
// pages. Fabricated endorsements are an FTC problem, not a copy nit.
//
// So: NO DATA, NO SECTION. Not a skeleton, not placeholder stars, not "reviews coming soon", not a
// dashed frame promising something. If src/data/generated/reviews.json is absent or empty this
// component returns nothing at all, and the page reads as complete without it. There is no sample
// review anywhere in this file, no default prop carrying a name, and nothing for a fixture to
// inject — `npm run verify` greps the built HTML for fixture content and this cannot trip it,
// because there is nothing to trip it with.
//
// A market with reviews but no profile figures shows its reviews and no aggregate. A market with
// nothing DROPS ITS COLUMN rather than showing a zero. Zeros are a claim too.
//
// ── GOOGLE'S TERMS, AS RENDERED HERE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Checked 2026-08-17 against
// https://developers.google.com/maps/documentation/places/web-service/policies:
//
//   · ATTRIBUTION IS REQUIRED: "always credit the author ... (author's avatar image, name, and
//     profile link)". Every card carries all three. A review missing any of them was dropped by
//     the pull rather than rendered anonymously here.
//   · NOT EDITED, NOT FILTERED, NOT REORDERED. Text is printed verbatim; the order is the order the
//     API returned. The policy requires a "clear notice that describes how reviews are being
//     ordered and filtered" — the note under the heading is that notice.
//   · AVATARS ARE REFERENCED, NEVER DOWNLOADED. The policy forbids caching Places content and is
//     silent on storing avatars, so the compliant reading is to point at Google's URL at render
//     time. `referrerpolicy="no-referrer"` and lazy loading keep that as cheap as possible.
import { readFileSync, existsSync } from "node:fs";
import { resolve } from "node:path";
import { MARKET_LIST } from "../data/markets.js";

// READ FROM DISK, NOT `import`. The generated file is optional by design — a fresh clone has no
// reviews and must still build — and `await import()` cannot express that: Rollup resolves imports
// statically, so a missing file is a build failure that no try/catch around the import can catch.
// It failed exactly that way the first time. readFileSync is checked at build run-time instead, so
// "no reviews yet" is an ordinary state rather than a broken build.
const FILE = resolve(process.cwd(), "src/data/generated/reviews.json");
let DATA = null;
if (existsSync(FILE)) {
  try {
    DATA = JSON.parse(readFileSync(FILE, "utf8"));
  } catch (e) {
    // A malformed file is louder than a missing one: it means the pull wrote something wrong, and
    // silently rendering no reviews would hide that. The build still completes.
    console.warn(`  ⚠ reviews.json could not be parsed — the reviews section will not render. ${e.message}`);
    DATA = null;
  }
}

// ── FIXTURE PREVIEW, QUARANTINED HARDER THAN THE DATA IT STANDS IN FOR ───────────────────────
//
// COLDSTREAM_FIXTURES=1 fills the section with synthetic reviews so the layout can be looked at
// before a Places key exists. Every name and every quote contains the string FIXTURE, and
// verify-build fails any build whose HTML contains it — "a build that shipped them would be
// publishing fabricated endorsements".
//
// IT IS AN ENV VAR AND NOT A PLACEHOLDER FILE, DELIBERATELY. The obvious approach is to write a
// placeholder generated/reviews.json, and that file is committed in normal use because the host
// builds from the repo. A placeholder version would then be one `git add -A` from production, and
// Netlify runs `npm run build`, not `npm run verify` — the gate would never see it. Nothing is
// written to disk here, so there is nothing to accidentally commit and nothing to clean up.
//
// It never overrides real data: if a pull has happened, the real file wins.
if (!DATA && process.env.COLDSTREAM_FIXTURES === "1") {
  const { SAMPLE_PLACES_REVIEWS } = await import("../data/fixtures/reviews-places.sample.js");
  console.warn("  ⚠⚠  FIXTURES ON — SYNTHETIC REVIEWS are being built into this site.");
  console.warn("      COLDSTREAM_FIXTURES=1. This output must never be deployed; npm run verify will refuse it.\n");
  DATA = SAMPLE_PLACES_REVIEWS;
}

const { heading = "What homeowners say, office by office" } = Astro.props;

// ON-PAGE, NOT JUST IN THE TERMINAL. A build warning is seen by the person who ran the build and
// nobody else. This section is going to be looked at by a team, screenshotted, and pasted into a
// deck — and a screenshot carries no console output. If the reviews are synthetic the page has to
// say so where the eye lands, or somebody will eventually show a client a fabricated 4.8 in good
// faith. It is the loudest thing in the section by design.
const IS_FIXTURE = DATA?.$fixture != null;

const columns = MARKET_LIST
  .map((m) => {
    const d = DATA?.markets?.[m.slug];
    const reviews = Array.isArray(d?.reviews) ? d.reviews : [];
    return {
      market: m,
      reviews,
      // Both fields, or no aggregate. `count` is the profile's total, not the number we show.
      aggregate:
        typeof d?.rating === "number" && Number.isInteger(d?.count) && d.count > 0
          ? { rating: d.rating, count: d.count, url: d.profileUrl ?? null }
          : null,
    };
  })
  .filter((c) => c.reviews.length > 0);

const show = columns.length > 0;

// Stars are drawn from the number. The number is also printed for a screen reader, because five
// glyphs are not a rating to anything that cannot see them.
const starsFor = (r) => {
  const full = Math.floor(r);
  const half = r - full >= 0.5;
  return { full, half, empty: 5 - full - (half ? 1 : 0) };
};
---
{show && (
  <section class="rbm" aria-labelledby="rbm-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="shead">
        <span class="eyebrow">Reviews</span>
        <h2 id="rbm-h">{heading}</h2>
        {/* The ordering-and-filtering notice Google's policy requires. It is also just true. */}
        <p class="rbm-note">
          Straight from each office's Google Business Profile, in the order Google returns them —
          nothing reordered, nothing left out. Google shows up to five per profile.
        </p>
      </div>

      {IS_FIXTURE && (
        <p class="rbm-fixture" role="status">
          <b>Placeholder reviews — not real.</b> Every name and quote below is invented, for reviewing
          the layout only. Real reviews arrive via <code>npm run reviews:pull</code> once a Google
          Places key exists. Do not screenshot this for a client.
        </p>
      )}

      <div class="rbm-cols">
        {columns.map((c, ci) => (
          <div class="rbm-col">
            <div class="rbm-office">
              <h3>{c.market.name}</h3>
              <p class="rbm-city">{c.market.office.city}, {c.market.office.state}</p>

              {c.aggregate ? (
                <p class="rbm-agg">
                  <span class="rbm-stars" aria-hidden="true">
                    {Array.from({ length: starsFor(c.aggregate.rating).full }).map(() => <span class="s-f">★</span>)}
                    {starsFor(c.aggregate.rating).half && <span class="s-h">★</span>}
                    {Array.from({ length: starsFor(c.aggregate.rating).empty }).map(() => <span class="s-e">★</span>)}
                  </span>
                  <span class="rbm-agg-txt">
                    {c.aggregate.url ? (
                      <a href={c.aggregate.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">
                        {c.aggregate.rating} from {c.aggregate.count} Google reviews
                      </a>
                    ) : (
                      <>{c.aggregate.rating} from {c.aggregate.count} Google reviews</>
                    )}
                  </span>
                </p>
              ) : (
                /* Reviews but no profile figures: show the reviews, claim no average. */
                <p class="rbm-agg rbm-agg-none">Reviews from this office's Google profile</p>
              )}
            </div>

            {/* THE MARQUEE. Columns are offset by index so the three do not move in lockstep, which
                is what makes it read as three separate profiles rather than one animation. The
                duplicate exists only to make the loop seamless and is aria-hidden — a screen reader
                gets each review once. Pauses on hover AND focus-within, so tabbing into a link
                stops the track rather than dragging the focused element out of view. */}
            <div class="rbm-track" style={`--rbm-delay:${ci * -7}s;--rbm-n:${c.reviews.length}`}>
              <ul class="rbm-list">
                {c.reviews.map((r) => (
                  <li class="rbm-card">
                    <div class="rbm-who">
                      {r.profile_photo_url && (
                        <img src={r.profile_photo_url} alt="" width="34" height="34"
                             loading="lazy" decoding="async" referrerpolicy="no-referrer" />
                      )}
                      <div>
                        <a class="rbm-name" href={r.author_url} target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">{r.name}</a>
                        {r.relative_time_description && <span class="rbm-when">{r.relative_time_description}</span>}
                      </div>
                    </div>
                    <p class="rbm-rating">
                      <span aria-hidden="true">
                        {Array.from({ length: starsFor(r.rating).full }).map(() => <span class="s-f">★</span>)}
                        {Array.from({ length: 5 - starsFor(r.rating).full }).map(() => <span class="s-e">★</span>)}
                      </span>
                      <span class="visually-hidden">{r.rating} out of 5</span>
                    </p>
                    {/* Verbatim. Astro escapes it; nothing here trims or truncates the text. */}
                    <blockquote>{r.quote}</blockquote>
                  </li>
                ))}
              </ul>
              <ul class="rbm-list" aria-hidden="true">
                {c.reviews.map((r) => (
                  <li class="rbm-card">
                    <div class="rbm-who">
                      {r.profile_photo_url && (
                        <img src={r.profile_photo_url} alt="" width="34" height="34"
                             loading="lazy" decoding="async" referrerpolicy="no-referrer" />
                      )}
                      <div>
                        <span class="rbm-name">{r.name}</span>
                        {r.relative_time_description && <span class="rbm-when">{r.relative_time_description}</span>}
                      </div>
                    </div>
                    <p class="rbm-rating">
                      <span>
                        {Array.from({ length: starsFor(r.rating).full }).map(() => <span class="s-f">★</span>)}
                        {Array.from({ length: 5 - starsFor(r.rating).full }).map(() => <span class="s-e">★</span>)}
                      </span>
                    </p>
                    <blockquote>{r.quote}</blockquote>
                  </li>
                ))}
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
)}
